{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-76013",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "At $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}h(x) & =h(2) \\\\  & ={3}^{c(2)} \\\\  & ={3}^{2c},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}({x}^{5}-5) \\\\  & ={2}^{5}-5 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2c} & =27 \\\\ {3}^{2c} & ={3}^{3} \\\\ 2c & =3 \\\\ c & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ for $c=\\frac{3}{2}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
